 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dd2LpIZoL2U/",
 "title": "2 ways to redirect someone who rambles",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator suggests two subtle ways to redirect a rambling person you don't know by finding a natural opening in their monologue. The goal is to build a bridge back to your own perspective and perspective on the conversation.",
 "key_points": [
  "Find an opportunity or window within the person's monologue.",
  "Say phrases like \"This reminds me of...\" or \"This sounds familiar to...\".",
  "Create a bridge from what they are talking about to what you want to say.",
  "Ensure the bridge makes sense in the context of the conversation.",
  "Example with cats: \"This reminds me of an article I read about cats and what it's like to live with certain pets, and I just don't know if it's for me. I don't know if I could do it.\"",
  "This helps steer the conversation back to your perspective and POV.",
  "Try these out before writing people off, as they might be unaware, lonely, or eager to talk."
 ],
 "content": "2 ways to redirect someone who rambles. I made a video last week teasing what it feels like to be in a conversation with someone who just doesn't stop talking. So, let's talk about some solutions. Now, if it's someone you don't know, one thing I'd recommend is to find some opportunity or window within their monologue and say, \"This reminds me of blank\" or \"This sounds familiar to blank.\" This creates a bridge from whatever it is that they won't stop talking about to whatever it is that you want to say. Just make sure the bridge makes sense. So if they're going on and on about their cat, for example, and this is no offense to my cat people out there, you can say something like, \"This reminds me of an article I read about cats\" and what it's like to live with certain pets, \"And I just don't know if it's for me. I don't know if I could do it.\" Do you see how you steered the conversation back to your perspective and your POV? Now, this isn't a guarantee that the conversation with the other person will become more well-balanced, but it's a start. Some people are just unaware, or maybe they're lonely and are eager to talk. So trying these out before shutting the door on them and saying, \"Well, it was nice talking to you, have a great night\" is worth a shot. I'm Well with Raele, here to help with your people skills and social confidence. This video got a little long, so I will do a part two about how to handle ramblers who you do actually know in conversation because that approach is slightly different. So, stick around for that. [Text on screen: 2 ways to redirect someone who rambles] [Text on screen: I'm Well With Raele] [Instagram post caption: 2 hacks for a subtle redirect 💬 Some people are unaware & need a little help. Others might just be lonely and eager to chat. Try these out before writing them off for good! #wellwithraele #socialskills #communication #tipsandtricks #socialetiquette]",
 "status": "done",
